            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array longer by adding values at its end - or, for a matrix, a row along the bottom.\n\nUse it to add a closing value, extend a sequence, or add a row to a table. To keep a growing record of a stream, np.rolling_buffer is usually better: it keeps the latest values at a fixed size instead of growing for ever.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.append <values: floats ...>\nDefault: no values (send them to the values inlet).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.append 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input has the values added and is sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nvalues:\nWhat to add, replacing any given as arguments. Held. A list is added element by element; a single row onto a matrix is added as a new row.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way: 0 (the default) at the end of a list, or as new rows of a matrix; 1 as new columns.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nappended:\nThe longer array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n4 5 added to the end of 1 2 3: 1 2 3 4 5. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.insert puts values in at any position. np.concatenate joins two arrays. np.rolling_buffer keeps the latest values of a stream.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 820,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes an array longer by adding values at its end - or, for a matrix, a row along the bottom.\n\nUse it to add a closing value, extend a sequence, or add a row to a table. To keep a growing record of a stream, np.rolling_buffer is usually better: it keeps the latest values at a fixed size instead of growing for ever.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.append <values: floats ...>\nDefault: no values (send them to the values inlet).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.append 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input has the values added and is sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nvalues:\nWhat to add, replacing any given as arguments. Held. A list is added element by element; a single row onto a matrix is added as a new row.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way: 0 (the default) at the end of a list, or as new rows of a matrix; 1 as new columns.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nappended:\nThe longer array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n4 5 added to the end of 1 2 3: 1 2 3 4 5. A metro re-sends the list twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.insert puts values in at any position. np.concatenate joins two arrays. np.rolling_buffer keeps the latest values of a stream."
        },
